---
name: array-indexed-store
description: "a(i) = v stores, on every lane and from every statement position"
backends: [int, js, jvm, v2]
---

# `a(i) = v` is `a.update(i, v)`

`BUGS.md` `v2-array-indexed-store-silently-dropped`. Scala defines an indexed store as
`a.update(i, v)`. Both self-hosted fronts failed to parse it and **dropped the store with no
diagnostic** — a wrong answer, not a crash, which is the failure shape this project treats as the
most expensive kind.

The two fronts failed for different reasons in different code, which is why the fix took two
commits: the legacy front reached assignment parsing and had no `app` arm there, while F's
assignment head is a two-token peek (identifier, then `=`), so `a(1) = 7` was never an assignment
head at all — the second token is `(`. It fell to expression parsing, which stops at `=` and
discards the rest.
The statement positions matter independently: F consults its head test in five places, and a fix
wired into one of them leaves the others silently dropping stores. A `def` body and a block
sequence are separate paths from the top level.

A computed index and a computed value, so the arm cannot be passing by folding literals — and a
second store to the same slot, so it is a real mutable cell rather than a fresh array each time.

```scalascript
val top = Array(0, 0, 0)
top(1) = 7
println(top(0))
println(top(1))
println(top(2))
def inDef(): Int =
  val a = Array(0, 0)
  a(1) = 5
  a(1)

def inSeq(): Int =
  val a = Array(0, 0)
  a(0) = 1
  a(1) = 2
  a(0) + a(1)

println(inDef())
println(inSeq())
def computed(): Int =
  val a = Array(0, 0, 0)
  val i = 1 + 1
  a(i) = 3 * 4
  a(i) = a(i) + 1
  a(i)

println(computed())
```
